/* Beat 4, against `Onboarding.dc.html` step 1. Composed onto ScreenScaffold
 * and out of Task 13's components; what is left here is the onboarding STEP
 * FRAME - the progress pips and the tip note - the card's own type ladder,
 * and the layout no component can know.
 *
 * NO PAGE HEADER. The handoff's step screen starts with the pips (line 33)
 * and its 26px Baloo heading (line 161) is inside the white card on line 158.
 * The scaffold is constructed with `title: null` and hides its header row;
 * FounderNamingView's class comment has what that costs.
 *
 * THIS FILE GREW IN PHASE 9 TASK 14 AND THAT IS NOT A COMPOSITION FAILURE.
 * The brief expected it to shrink, on the general rule that a screen reaching
 * for the component vocabulary should be deleting what it used to hand-roll.
 * Phase 8 Task 9 had already done that deletion - the four rules it removed
 * are listed below and none came back - so there was nothing of that kind
 * left to take out. What arrived instead is the step frame: five pips, a step
 * counter and a tip note, which exist on this screen because the handoff's
 * onboarding has them and on no other screen in the app. A component for a
 * vocabulary of one is the trade Task 13 spent its whole brief declining, and
 * `HeroSlot`, `SectionCard` and `ScreenScaffold` are what this screen DID
 * take instead of deriving: the ring, the disc, the species tint, the card,
 * the elevation wrapper, the header, the CTA row. Those are the deletions;
 * they happened one phase early.
 *
 * FOUR RULES LEFT THIS FILE IN PHASE 8 TASK 9 AND NONE OF THEM WERE
 * REPLACED BY A COPY HERE, which is the whole test of whether a screen is
 * composing the layer or re-deriving it:
 *   - the root's `padding: var(--gutter)` is the scaffold's content region.
 *   - `__prompt`'s colour and weight are Theme's own `Label` rule. Its SIZE
 *     is set below, because the handoff's body copy is 13px and the default
 *     Label is 12. It carried `.t-section` (19px Baloo) until Task 14b, on
 *     the understanding that the screen title had moved to the scaffold's
 *     header - which the handoff does not do. The title is `__card-title`
 *     in the same card now and the prompt is the body line under it.
 *   - `__blocker`'s fill, radius and padding are Theme's `.panel-coral` and
 *     its ink is `.t-danger`.
 *   - `__skip`'s whole treatment was a verbatim second copy of Theme's
 *     `.btn-quiet`, down to the `:active` rule.
 *   - `__confirm` and `__name`'s margins are the CTA row's padding and
 *     Theme's own TextField margin.
 */
.founder-naming-view { flex-grow: 1; }

/* ------------------------------------------------------- the step frame */

/* THE PIPS ARE A FULL-WIDTH ROW ABOVE THE HERO, WHICH IS WHERE THE HANDOFF
 * DRAWS THEM. `Onboarding.dc.html:34-40` is a row of five `flex: 1` bars with
 * the step label at the end, `padding: 10px 16px 12px`, with nothing above it
 * but the status bar.
 *
 * TASK 14 PUT THEM IN THE SCAFFOLD'S HEADER SLOT AND SHRANK THEM TO FIT.
 * That was the right call while this screen still had a page header - a
 * titles column carrying `flex-grow: 1` was beside them, so five stretching
 * bars had nothing to stretch into and became five fixed 8px marks. With no
 * header there is no column to compete with: the row is a child of the
 * content region and the pips take the handoff's own `flex: 1`.
 *
 * `flex-shrink: 0` SO IT KEEPS ITS HEIGHT. This is a child of a column whose
 * other children grow; a 4px row is the first thing a shrink pass would take.
 *
 * --space-2 IS 8px AGAINST THE HANDOFF'S 10px TOP PADDING, and --space-1 is
 * 4 against its 12px below - the hero's elevation wrapper contributes 6px of
 * its own above the card's white edge, so the measured gap is 10. Both on the
 * scale. */
.founder-naming-view__progress {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
}

/* 2px is half of --space-1 (4px), which is this element's own height - the
 * literal-beside-the-measurement form Tokens.uss's radius block requires,
 * because there is no --radius-pill and a pill radius is a function of one
 * element's height. ROUNDED DOWN IS NOT NEEDED HERE: 4 is even, so 2 is
 * exactly half and the cap is a true semicircle.
 *
 * --hairline IS THE UNLIT STATE AND IT IS A DELIBERATE STEP DOWN FROM THE
 * HANDOFF, which uses #e3dbf3. That colour is not in the token layer and is
 * 22 in summed channel distance from --hairline (#ece7f6), the token this
 * project already uses for exactly this job - an inactive outline on paper.
 * Inventing a second near-identical grey-violet to be faithful to one
 * gradient stop is the drift verify-uss-tokens.sh exists to stop. */
/* `flex-grow` AND A ZERO BASIS IS THE HANDOFF'S `flex: 1`, WRITTEN OUT.
 * USS has no `flex` shorthand's three-value form to lean on here and a bar
 * with an `auto` basis would size to nothing and then distribute, which
 * happens to give the same answer only because these five have no content.
 * Stated so a sixth pip with a label in it cannot quietly change the widths.
 *
 * --space-2 IS THE GAP, 8px against the handoff's 9. On the scale. It is on
 * every pip including the last, which is also the gap to the step counter -
 * the handoff's own row has one uniform 9px gap the whole way across, so the
 * counter sets no left margin of its own. */
.progress-pip {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    height: var(--space-1);
    border-radius: 2px;
    background-color: var(--hairline);
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
}

/* The handoff lights every bar up to and including the current step. Beat 4
 * is step 1, so exactly one is lit and this modifier is on exactly one pip
 * in the UXML. --violet is the handoff's own `ON` (#7a6ac0), to the digit. */
.progress-pip--current { background-color: var(--violet); }

/* "1 / 5". The handoff sets it at 10.5px in #a29bb5, which is --mute
 * exactly; the size comes from `.t-num` at --text-secondary (11px), because
 * bible 10.6 puts an 11px floor under any number a decision depends on and
 * "how much of this is left" is one. The half-pixel is recorded here rather
 * than argued with, on StatCell.uss's precedent.
 *
 * NO LEFT MARGIN, AND THAT IS A DELETION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. It carried
 * --space-1 while the last pip carried --space-1 on its right; both together
 * made 8px in a row whose gaps were 4. The pips now carry --space-2 and the
 * handoff's row has ONE uniform gap across all six children, so a second
 * margin here would make the counter's gap twice every other one.
 *
 * `flex-shrink: 0` IS REACHABLE NOW IN A WAY IT WAS NOT BEFORE. The pips
 * carry `flex-basis: 0`, so on a frame narrow enough for the row to overflow
 * they have nothing left to give and the shrink falls entirely on this label.
 * With the old fixed-width pips the same overflow was shared. Four glyphs
 * that say which step this is must not be what gives way. */
.founder-naming-view__step {
    color: var(--mute);
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------- the screen */

/* THE HERO REGION IS A BAND, NOT A BADGE, AND 300 IS THE HANDOFF'S OWN
 * NUMBER. `Onboarding.dc.html`'s hero is `flex: 1; min-height: 300px` - it
 * takes whatever the column has left and never goes under 300 - and what
 * fills it is a dotted ring, a glowing disc and the animal inside them. That
 * IS a `HeroSlot`, which is why Task 13 built one; the slot is 96px because
 * --hero-slot is a token three files agree on, so the SIZE of the region has
 * to come from the card around it rather than from the component in it.
 * Without this the founder sat in a 130px strip and read as a list thumbnail
 * on a screen whose whole job is to look at one creature.
 *
 * `flex-grow` IS THE `flex: 1` HALF AND IT IS LIVE AS OF TASK 14b. It was
 * dead when it was written: the scaffold's content region is a ScrollView,
 * and a ScrollView's content container is sized by its content so that it can
 * exceed the viewport, so a growing child inside it had nothing to grow into
 * and this screen ended in ~180px of bare --paper. That was a scaffold fault
 * and it was fixed in the scaffold - `.screen-scaffold__content
 * .unity-scroll-view__content-container` now carries `min-height: 100%`, and
 * that rule's note has why a minimum rather than a height. This declaration
 * is the handoff's `flex: 1`, not a speculative marker: the hero takes
 * whatever the column has left, and the band closes because the hero grew
 * into it rather than because anything was padded out to hide it.
 *
 * `justify-content: center` ON THE SURFACE, so the slot sits in the middle
 * of the band rather than at the top of it. This reaches into SectionCard's
 * own element, which is the direction a screen's sheet is allowed to reach -
 * downward into its descendants, never upward (StatCell.uss's closing note).
 */
.founder-naming-view__hero { flex-grow: 1; }

/* THE SURFACE HAS TO GROW TOO, AND THE FIRST CAPTURE AFTER THE SCAFFOLD FIX
 * IS WHAT SAID SO. `flex-grow` on the rule above is on the component ROOT,
 * which is the `.elev-1` WRAPPER - so the wrapper stretched to ~460px and the
 * white surface stayed at its 300px minimum, leaving the nine-sliced shadow
 * drawn around 160px of bare --paper. Measured in that capture: x=10 held the
 * falloff's 245,242,249 all the way to y=505 while x=215 was 247,244,251
 * - paper - from y=352 down.
 *
 * `.section-card` IS A COLUMN, so a growing child fills it. This reaches into
 * SectionCard's own element, which is the direction a screen's sheet is
 * allowed to reach - downward into its descendants, never upward (StatCell
 * .uss's closing note).
 *
 * THE `min-height` STAYS AND IS NOT NOW REDUNDANT. It is the handoff's floor
 * (Onboarding.dc.html:43, `flex: 1; min-height: 300px`) and it is what this
 * band measures on any frame short enough that there is no slack to grow
 * into - a smaller phone, or this screen with the checklist and reward cards
 * the handoff draws under its own step card. */
.founder-naming-view__hero .section-card__surface {
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 300px;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* The hero card holds one HeroSlot, which is a fixed 96px square - so the
 * centring is the container's job and cannot come from the slot. A
 * SectionCard body is a column at the default `align-items: stretch`, and a
 * fixed-width child in a stretch column is left-aligned rather than
 * stretched, which is why this rule is still needed after the CreatureCard
 * it used to centre became a HeroSlot. */
.founder-naming-view__founder { align-items: center; }

/* ------------------------------------------ the card's own type ladder */

/* THREE LINES IN ONE SURFACE, WHICH IS THE CORRECTION TASK 14b EXISTS FOR.
 * `Onboarding.dc.html` lines 160-162 are a 9px uppercase `.lbl` kicker, a
 * 26px Baloo 700 title 3px under it, and 13px 700 body copy 7px under that -
 * all three inside the white card on line 158. Task 14 put the first two in
 * the scaffold's page header and left the third here, so a 21px page title
 * and a 19px card line competed across two containers. One ladder, one card.
 *
 * THE KICKER HAS NO RULE IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `.t-micro` in
 * the UXML is the whole of it - 10px against the handoff's 9, --mute is
 * #a29bb5 to the digit, and the 1px `letter-spacing` is its `.1em`. It is the
 * card's first child, so it needs no margin either. A `flex-shrink: 0` was
 * written here and taken out again: this is a column whose height nothing
 * constrains, so it changed no pixel, which is this project's own test for a
 * rule that was only ever restating the layout engine.
 *
 * TWO CLASSES ON THE CARD TITLE, to beat `.t-section`'s own `font-size`
 * deterministically
 * rather than by sheet order - the override form every component sheet in
 * this project uses. What is taken from `.t-section` is the Baloo 2 face,
 * --ink and the bold flag; only the SIZE is this screen's.
 *
 * --text-card-hero IS 26px AND IS NOT A BIGGER PAGE TITLE. Tokens.uss has
 * the measurement and the source line. The scaffold's --text-screen-title
 * stays at 21px, which is what every handoff screen that HAS a header sets
 * its own title to; this screen has none.
 *
 * `white-space: normal` because "Name your Founder" fits on one line at 26px
 * in a 366px card and a longer title from a later build would not. */
.founder-naming-view .founder-naming-view__card-title {
    font-size: var(--text-card-hero);
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
    white-space: normal;
}

/* THE BODY LINE. The handoff sets it at 13px weight 700 in #5d5670;
 * --text-card-title is 13px to the digit and the face and weight are Theme's
 * own `Label` rule (Nunito-Bold, which IS the 700 face - that file's note has
 * why no `-unity-font-style: bold` goes with it).
 *
 * --ink RATHER THAN THE HANDOFF'S #5d5670, AND IT IS A REAL DIFFERENCE
 * RATHER THAN A ROUNDING. #5d5670 is 88 from --ink and 207 from --mute in
 * summed channel distance - it is a near-neutral step BETWEEN them that the
 * token layer does not hold. Recorded as a residual rather than closed with a
 * new colour: the same near-neutral is the handoff's `noteText` (line 245)
 * and Task 14 already took --violet-text there for the same reason, so
 * inventing it now would be one token for two call sites that both already
 * read a token. --ink is one step darker, which on 13px body copy inside a
 * white card is the safe direction.
 *
 * --space-2 is 8px against the handoff's 7px `margin-top`. On the scale. */
.founder-naming-view__prompt {
    font-size: var(--text-card-title);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    white-space: normal;
}

/* A sentence, not a label, so it wraps. Shown only when it says something -
 * FounderNamingView.Blocker has the measurement. */
.founder-naming-view__blocker {
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
}

/* THE TIP NOTE, WHICH USED TO BE THE SCAFFOLD'S FOOTER NOTE. Same sentence,
 * moved: `Onboarding.dc.html` step 1 puts its note INSIDE the card, on
 * `background: #f1ecfa; border-radius: 14px; padding: 10px 12px; margin-top:
 * 13px`, behind a 24px white disc holding a check. The fill, radius and
 * padding are `.panel-violet`'s - --violet-tint is #f1ecfa to the digit and
 * --radius-row is 14px to the digit - so this rule sets only the row
 * direction and the gap above.
 *
 * IT HAS TO BE ON WHITE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS INSIDE THE SectionCard AND NOT
 * UNDER IT. Measured in summed channel distance: --violet-tint against
 * --surface is 38, against --paper it is 15. Fifteen is the neighbourhood
 * the Pale hero disc was just moved OUT of (1157ed2 - four, and the disc read
 * as a bare ring on the page it was drawn on). A tip panel that only exists
 * as a faint wash is a tip panel nobody reads, so it sits on the card like
 * the handoff's does.
 *
 * --space-3 IS 12px AGAINST THE HANDOFF'S 13. On the scale; the 1px is not
 * worth a one-use token, on this project's standing convention for one-off
 * geometry. */
.founder-naming-view__note {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
}

/* 12px is half of 24 - a square element, so both axes clamp alike and this
 * is a true circle (Theme.uss header item 4). The handoff's gap to the copy
 * is 10px; --space-2 is 8, on the scale.
 *
 * THE THIRD COPY OF THIS DISC IN THE PROJECT, after `.mutation-banner__disc`
 * (24px) and `.resource-pill__disc` (22px). Not unified, deliberately: the
 * three differ in size, tint and glyph, and the shared part is six
 * declarations of circle arithmetic that a component wrapping them would
 * have to parameterise all of. Task 13 drew the line at nine components and
 * this is on the far side of it. */
.founder-naming-view__note-disc {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    background-color: var(--surface);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
}

/* TWO CLASSES, to beat icons.uss's `.icon` (19px, --mute-soft) without
 * depending on which sheet the panel applies last - the same override form
 * `.mutation-banner__spark` and `.resource-pill__glyph` are written in. The
 * handoff draws this mark at 12px in the 24px disc.
 *
 * --violet-text IS MEASURED AGAINST THE DISC IT IS ACTUALLY DRAWN ON, which
 * is --surface and not the --violet-tint panel behind it - the mistake the
 * retired --amber-bright-deep was built on and that Tokens.uss's --amber-text
 * note records. On --surface it is 5.49:1, well clear of the 3:1 WCAG floor
 * a tinted graphic needs. It is also `.t-violet`'s colour on the copy two
 * words to its right, so the mark and the sentence are one ink. The
 * handoff's own stroke here is #7a6ac0, one step brighter; --violet-text is
 * the token this project already reads for violet ink on light. */
.founder-naming-view .founder-naming-view__note-glyph {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--violet-text);
}

/* --violet-text comes from `.t-violet`; 11.5px in the handoff, and
 * --text-secondary (11px) is the nearest step on the scale. `white-space:
 * normal` so the caveat wraps rather than running off the panel - the
 * mistake `.screen-scaffold__footer-note` records making once, on this very
 * sentence, in the row this one replaced. */
.founder-naming-view__note-text {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    white-space: normal;
    font-size: var(--text-secondary);
}
